Crash-proof relay demo
======================

Demonstrates that a stateful relay server (tcpr-bridge) can CRASH and RECOVER
mid-transfer without either client (plain `nc`, no TCPR awareness) noticing,
and proves no data was lost with an end-to-end checksum.

Topology (see ../recover for the same style):

    [ peer netns 192.0.2.2 ]  <--bridge-->  [ application netns 192.0.2.1 ]
      nc sender / receiver        TCPR           tcpr-bridge (recoverable)

Run in a VM (recommended)
-------------------------

TCPR loads an experimental netfilter module; a bug could panic the kernel.
Run the whole demo inside a throwaway KVM guest so only the guest is at risk.

One-time host prereqs (need sudo):

  sudo apt-get install -y qemu-system-x86 qemu-utils cloud-image-utils
  sudo usermod -aG kvm "$USER"      # then log out/in (or: newgrp kvm)

Boot the guest (downloads a Debian 12 cloud image on first run):

  ./vm-setup                        # serial console; login: debian / tcpr

cloud-init auto-builds + installs TCPR inside the guest (against the GUEST
kernel) from this repo, shared read-only over 9p. When /root/TCPR_READY
exists, run the demo inside the guest:

  sudo -i
  cd /root/tcpr/examples/relay
  # follow "Running on bare metal" below (modprobe tcpr already works)

  ./vm-destroy                      # remove the VM disk when finished


Running on bare metal
---------------------

Prerequisites: the userspace tools are built and installed (see ../../README),
and you are root.

The kernel module must match the RUNNING kernel. If `modinfo tcpr` is missing
or shows the wrong vermagic, (re)build and install it first:

  sudo ./install-module       # builds tcpr.ko for `uname -r`, installs, depmod

Steps (use several terminals):

  # terminal 0 (host)
  ./setup-network
  ./setup-tcpr                # loads the module (modprobe) + installs rules
  ./make-payload 200M        # writes /tmp/in.dat and prints its sha256

  # terminal 1 - the recoverable relay
  ./server

  # terminal 2 - receiver client (connect FIRST)
  ./recv

  # terminal 3 - sender client
  ./send

  # ---- MID-TRANSFER: kill the relay in terminal 1 (^C), then: ----
  ./recover                  # tcpr-bridge reloads its state file and resumes

  # optional, in a spare terminal: watch the state change live
  ./watch-state

  # when the transfer finishes:
  ./verify                   # PASS => /tmp/out.dat is byte-identical to /tmp/in.dat

Cleanup:

  ./teardown-tcpr
  ./teardown-network

Notes:
  - tcpr-bridge persists connection metadata to
    /var/tmp/tcpr-bridge-192.0.2.1:8888.state; teardown-network removes it.
  - The clients are unmodified `nc` - the whole point is peer transparency.
  - `watch-state` uses the control tools: tcpr-get | tcpr-print.
